.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="faradays_law_mass gives the mass of a species deposited or consumed by a constant current over time. This example verifies the linear proportionality to both current and time (Faraday&#x27;s first law), computes the minimum applied voltage needed to force a non-spontaneous (electrolytic) reaction to run, and contrasts a spontaneous galvanic reaction against one that needs external electrolysis.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/electrochem/electrolysis/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_faradays_laws_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/electrochem/electrolysis/plot_01_faradays_laws`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Faraday's laws of electrolysis, and galvanic vs. electrolytic cells</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/electrochem/electrolysis/plot_01_faradays_laws`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Days after learning of Volta&#x27;s newly announced pile, William Nicholson and Anthony Carlisle connected one to a bowl of water in May 1800 and watched gas bubble from each wire -- the first electrolysis, and the first new discovery the pile made possible. This example reconstructs both halves of that observation from functions already in this package: the theoretical minimum decomposition voltage of water (~chemistrykit.electrochem.systems.electrolysis.minimum_applied_voltage_electrolytic, applied to the tabulated H+/H2 and O2/H2O standard potentials) comes out at the textbook 1.23 V, and Faraday&#x27;s law (~chemistrykit.electrochem.systems.electrolysis.moles_from_charge) recovers the 2:1 hydrogen-to-oxygen ratio Nicholson and Carlisle actually saw bubbling from the two wires, directly from electron-transfer stoichiometry.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/electrochem/electrolysis/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_02_water_electrolysis_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/electrochem/electrolysis/plot_02_water_electrolysis`

.. raw:: html

